{% comment %}
  vaidyam-article-faqs.liquid — Clinical FAQ Accordion
{% endcomment %}

<section class="ka-article-faq ka-blog-section--tight" id="faq" aria-labelledby="ka-faq-title" style="margin: 40px 0;">
  <p class="vaidyam-eyebrow">PRACTITIONER Q&amp;A</p>
  <h2 id="ka-faq-title" class="ka-article-faq-title" style="font-family: 'Amstir', serif; font-size: 26px; color: #1B4332; margin-bottom: 24px;">Frequently Asked Clinical Questions</h2>
  
  <div class="vaidyam-faq-accordion" style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px;">
    <details class="ka-faq-item" style="background: #FAF8F4; border: 1px solid #ECE7DB; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px 22px;">
      <summary class="ka-faq-summary" style="font-weight: 700; color: #1B4332; font-size: 16px; cursor: pointer;">
        <span>What is the optimal timing for Triphala administration (Morning vs. Bedtime)?</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ka-faq-answer" style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 15px; color: #555; line-height: 1.6;">
        <p>When prescribed as a <em>Virechana / Anulomana</em> for bowel regulation, administration at bedtime with warm water is optimal. When prescribed as a <em>Rasayana</em> for metabolic correction and tissue rejuvenation, morning administration on an empty stomach with honey (for Kapha) or ghee (for Pitta) is recommended according to classical references (Charaka Chikitsa 1:3).</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="ka-faq-item" style="background: #FAF8F4; border: 1px solid #ECE7DB; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px 22px;">
      <summary class="ka-faq-summary" style="font-weight: 700; color: #1B4332; font-size: 16px; cursor: pointer;">
        <span>Can Triphala be safely prescribed to patients with Pitta-dominant GERD or ulcers?</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ka-faq-answer" style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 15px; color: #555; line-height: 1.6;">
        <p>Yes, but the Anupana must be modified. In active Pitta aggravation or hyperacidity, combine Triphala Churna with <em>Ghrita</em> (clarified butter) or cold infusion (Hima Kalpana) to mitigate the mild Ushna virya of Haritaki and Bibhitaki while leveraging the Sheeta virya and Pittahara potency of Amalaki.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="ka-faq-item" style="background: #FAF8F4; border: 1px solid #ECE7DB; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px 22px;">
      <summary class="ka-faq-summary" style="font-weight: 700; color: #1B4332; font-size: 16px; cursor: pointer;">
        <span>What are the contraindications for Triphala therapy?</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ka-faq-answer" style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 15px; color: #555; line-height: 1.6;">
        <p>Triphala should be avoided during pregnancy (due to mild downward laxative effect on Apana Vayu), acute diarrhea, severe dehydration, and in individuals with extreme Rukshata (dryness / emaciation) without an unctuous Anupana.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="ka-faq-item" style="background: #FAF8F4; border: 1px solid #ECE7DB; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px 22px;">
      <summary class="ka-faq-summary" style="font-weight: 700; color: #1B4332; font-size: 16px; cursor: pointer;">
        <span>How does Triphala affect the intestinal microbiome based on recent studies?</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ka-faq-answer" style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 15px; color: #555; line-height: 1.6;">
        <p>Recent studies (Peterson et al., 2017) show that Triphala's polyphenols stimulate the growth of beneficial gut bacteria including <em>Bifidobacterium</em> and <em>Lactobacillus</em> species while inhibiting opportunistic pathogens like <em>Escherichia coli</em>, facilitating short-chain fatty acid (SCFA) synthesis.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
